}
```

Both documents converge after each sync round. Shape additions, moves, and color changes from either peer appear in both documents.

# Step 5: Canvas Rendering

The Avalonia Canvas renders shapes using Rectangle and Ellipse controls positioned with `Canvas.SetLeft` and `Canvas.SetTop`:

```csharp
  void AddShapeToCanvas(ShapeViewModel shape)
  {
    Control shapeControl = CreateShapeControl(shape);
    Canvas.SetLeft(shapeControl, shape.X);
    Canvas.SetTop(shapeControl, shape.Y);
    _canvas.Children.Add(shapeControl);
  }

  Control CreateShapeControl(ShapeViewModel shape)
  {
    var brush = new SolidColorBrush(Color.Parse(shape.Color));
    Control inner = shape.Type == ShapeType.Ellipse
      ? new Ellipse { Width = shape.Width, Height = shape.Height, Fill = brush }
      : new Rectangle { Width = shape.Width, Height = shape.Height, Fill = brush };

    return new Border { Child = inner, Tag = shape };
  }
```

Pointer events handle drawing new shapes (click-drag on empty canvas), moving existing shapes (click-drag on shape), and selection (click on shape shows selection border).

# Key APIs Used

| API | Purpose |
|-----|---------|
| `PutDouble(id, key, value)` | Set x, y, width, height coordinates |
| `GetDouble(id, key)` | Read coordinate values |
| `PutBoolean(id, key, value)` | Set layer visibility |
| `GetBoolean(id, key)` | Read visibility state |
| `PutObject(id, key, Map/List)` | Create shape maps, layer maps, shape lists |
| `ListInsertObject(list, idx, Map)` | Add shapes and layers |
| `ListRemove(list, idx)` | Remove shapes and layers |
| `Fork()` | Create undo snapshot with shared document history |
| `Save()` / `Load(bytes)` | Persist and restore canvas state |
| `GenerateSyncMessage(state)` | Produce sync data for peer |
| `ReceiveSyncMessage(state, msg)` | Apply sync data from peer |
| `CreateSyncState()` | Initialize per-peer sync tracking |

# Overview

This walkthrough builds a Kanban-style task board desktop application using Automerge CRDTs. It demonstrates structured data operations: maps for columns and tasks, lists for ordering, and counters for vote tracking.

The finished app displays three default columns (To Do, In Progress, Done) with the ability to add columns, add tasks, remove items, upvote tasks, and move tasks between columns.

# Architecture

```
BoardViewModel
  └── AutomergeService
        └── AutomergeDocument
              └── root (map)
                    └── "columns" (list)
                          └── column (map)
                                ├── "name", "order"
                                └── "tasks" (list)
                                      └── task (map)
                                            ├── "title", "description", "status"
                                            └── "votes" (counter)
```

**AutomergeService** wraps an `AutomergeDocument` and exposes typed CRUD operations for columns and tasks.
**BoardViewModel** bridges the service layer to the Avalonia UI with observable collections and commands.

# Step 1: Project Setup

Create an Avalonia Desktop project that references the Automerge libraries:

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="11.3.6"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="11.3.6"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="11.3.6"/>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.ReactiveUI" Version="11.3.6"/>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\libs\Ray.Automerge\Ray.Automerge.csproj"/>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\libs\Ray.Automerge.Repo\Ray.Automerge.Repo.csproj"/>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
```

# Step 2: AutomergeService

The service initializes the document with a `columns` list at the root and provides typed operations:

```csharp
sealed class AutomergeService : IDisposable
{
  readonly AutomergeDocument _document;
  readonly AutomergeObjectId _columnsListId;

  public AutomergeService()
  {
    _document = AutomergeDocument.Create();
    _columnsListId = _document.PutObject(
      _document.RootId, "columns", AutomergeContainerType.List);
    _document.Commit("Initialize board");
  }

  public Column AddColumn(string name)
  {
    ulong index = _document.ListLength(_columnsListId);
    var columnId = _document.ListInsertObject(
      _columnsListId, index, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
    _document.PutString(columnId, "name", name);
    _document.PutInt64(columnId, "order", (long)index);
    var tasksListId = _document.PutObject(
      columnId, "tasks", AutomergeContainerType.List);
    _document.Commit($"Add column: {name}");
    // return Column model...
  }
}
```

Key patterns:

- `PutObject(root, "columns", List)` creates the top-level columns list.
- `ListInsertObject(list, index, Map)` appends a new column as a map at the end of the list.
- `PutObject(column, "tasks", List)` nests a tasks list inside each column map.

# Step 3: Task CRUD

Adding a task inserts a new map into a column's tasks list:

```csharp
public TaskItem AddTask(
  AutomergeObjectId tasksListId,
  string title,
  string description,
  string status)
{
  ulong index = _document.ListLength(tasksListId);
  var taskId = _document.ListInsertObject(
    tasksListId, index, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
  _document.PutString(taskId, "title", title);
  _document.PutString(taskId, "description", description);
  _document.PutString(taskId, "status", status);
  _document.PutCounter(taskId, "votes", 0);
  _document.Commit($"Add task: {title}");
  // return TaskItem model...
}
```

The `PutCounter` call initializes a counter CRDT at the `"votes"` key. Counters automatically merge concurrent increments without conflicts.

# Step 4: Counter Increments

Upvoting a task uses the `Increment` operation:

```csharp
public void IncrementVotes(AutomergeObjectId taskId, long delta = 1)
{
  _document.Increment(taskId, "votes", delta);
  _document.Commit("Vote");
}
```

Unlike `PutInt64` which would cause last-writer-wins conflicts, `Increment` is commutative: if two peers both upvote concurrently, both increments are preserved after sync.

# Step 5: Moving Tasks

Moving a task between columns requires removing from the source list and inserting into the destination:

```csharp
public TaskItem MoveTask(
  AutomergeObjectId sourceTasksListId,
  ulong sourceIndex,
  AutomergeObjectId destTasksListId,
  ulong destIndex,
  TaskItem task)
{
  _document.ListRemove(sourceTasksListId, sourceIndex);

  var newTaskId = _document.ListInsertObject(
    destTasksListId, destIndex, AutomergeContainerType.Map);
  _document.PutString(newTaskId, "title", task.Title);
  _document.PutString(newTaskId, "description", task.Description);
  _document.PutString(newTaskId, "status", task.Status);
  _document.PutCounter(newTaskId, "votes", task.Votes);
  _document.Commit($"Move task: {task.Title}");
  // return new TaskItem...
}
```

Since Automerge does not support moving objects between lists natively, the move is implemented as a delete-and-reinsert. The task data is copied to a new map in the destination list.

# Step 6: Reading the Board

The `ReadBoard` method iterates the document structure to produce a list of columns with their tasks:

```csharp
public List<Column> ReadBoard()
{
  var columns = new List<Column>();
  ulong columnCount = _document.ListLength(_columnsListId);

  for(ulong i = 0; i < columnCount; i++)
  {
    var columnId = _document.ListGetObject(_columnsListId, i);
    var tasksListId = _document.GetObject(columnId, "tasks");
    var column = new Column
    {
      ObjectId = columnId,
      TasksListId = tasksListId,
      Name = _document.GetString(columnId, "name") ?? string.Empty,
      Order = _document.GetInt64(columnId, "order") ?? 0,
    };

    ulong taskCount = _document.ListLength(tasksListId);
    for(ulong j = 0; j < taskCount; j++)
    {
      var taskId = _document.ListGetObject(tasksListId, j);
      column.Tasks.Add(new TaskItem
      {
        ObjectId = taskId,
        Title = _document.GetString(taskId, "title") ?? string.Empty,
        Votes = _document.GetCounter(taskId, "votes") ?? 0,
        // ... other properties
      });
    }

    columns.Add(column);
  }

  return columns;
}
```

# Step 7: View

The XAML uses a horizontal `ItemsControl` for columns, each containing a vertical task list:

```xml
<ScrollViewer HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Columns}">
    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
        <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8"/>
      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:ColumnViewModel">
        <Border Background="#F5F5F5" CornerRadius="8" Width="300">
          <DockPanel>
            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Name}" FontWeight="SemiBold"/>
            <Button Content="+ Add Task" DockPanel.Dock="Bottom"/>
            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Tasks}">
              <!-- Task card template with title, status, vote button -->
            </ItemsControl>
          </DockPanel>
        </Border>
      </DataTemplate>
    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
  </ItemsControl>
</ScrollViewer>
```

Each task card displays the title, status, a vote button with counter, and a delete button.

# APIs Demonstrated

| API | Purpose |
|-----|---------|
| `PutObject(id, key, Map)` | Create column and task maps |
| `PutObject(id, key, List)` | Create ordered task lists |
| `PutString(id, key, value)` | Set text properties |
| `PutInt64(id, key, value)` | Set numeric properties |
| `PutCounter(id, key, value)` | Initialize a counter |
| `Increment(id, key, delta)` | Increment a counter |
| `ListInsertObject(list, idx, Map)` | Add items to lists |
| `ListRemove(list, idx)` | Remove items from lists |
| `ListLength(list)` | Get list size |
| `ListGetObject(list, idx)` | Read nested objects |
| `GetString(id, key)` | Read string properties |
| `GetInt64(id, key)` | Read integer properties |
| `GetCounter(id, key)` | Read counter value |
| `GetObject(id, key)` | Navigate nested containers |
| `Commit(message)` | Commit pending changes |
| `Save()` / `Load(bytes)` | Full document persistence |

# Running the App

```bash
dotnet run --project apps/DemoApp.TaskBoard
```

The board starts with three default columns: To Do, In Progress, and Done. Use the toolbar buttons to add columns, click "+ Add Task" to add tasks to columns, click the up arrow to upvote, and click "X" to remove items.

# Overview

This guide walks you through installing Ray.Toolkit.Automerge and creating your first document.

You'll learn how to create a document, add data, commit changes, and view the results.

# Installation

Add the NuGet package to your project using the .NET CLI:

```bash
dotnet add package Ray.Automerge
```

Or using the Package Manager Console in Visual Studio:

```powershell
Install-Package Ray.Automerge
```

# Requirements

The library requires .NET 9.0 or later.

It includes native binaries for Windows, Linux, and macOS. The correct native library loads automatically based on your platform.

# Your First Document

Let's create a simple document that stores user profile information.

## Creating a Document

Start by creating a new Automerge document:

```csharp
using Ray.Automerge;

using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
```

The document implements `IDisposable`. Use a `using` statement to ensure proper cleanup of native resources.

## Getting the Root Object

Every document has a root object where you store data:

```csharp
var root = doc.RootId;
```

The root is a Map container. You can add key-value pairs to it.

## Adding Data

Add different types of data to the root map:

```csharp
doc.PutString(root, "username", "alice");
doc.PutInt64(root, "age", 30);
doc.PutBoolean(root, "is_active", true);
doc.PutTimestamp(root, "created_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
```

The library supports multiple data types:
- Strings
- Integers (Int64, UInt64)
- Floating-point numbers (Double)
- Booleans
- Timestamps (Unix milliseconds)
- Bytes
- Null values
- Counters (CRDT counters)
- Nested containers (Map, List, Text)

## Committing Changes

Changes remain pending until you commit them:

```csharp
var commitHash = doc.Commit("Add user profile");
```

The commit message helps track what changed. The method returns a hash identifying this commit.

Automerge tracks all changes. You can roll back uncommitted changes using `doc.Rollback()`.

## Viewing the Data

Convert the document to JSON to see the current state:

```csharp
var json = doc.HydrateJson();
Console.WriteLine(json);
```

This outputs something like:

```json
{
  "username": "alice",
  "age": 30,
  "is_active": true,
  "created_at": 1234567890
}
```

# Complete Example

Here's a complete program that creates a user profile:

```csharp
using System;
using Ray.Automerge;

using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
var root = doc.RootId;

// Add user data
doc.PutString(root, "username", "alice");
doc.PutString(root, "email", "alice@example.com");
doc.PutInt64(root, "age", 30);
doc.PutBoolean(root, "email_verified", true);
doc.PutTimestamp(root, "joined_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());

// Create a nested preferences object
var preferences = doc.PutObject(root, "preferences", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
doc.PutString(preferences, "theme", "dark");
doc.PutBoolean(preferences, "notifications", true);

// Commit the changes
var hash = doc.Commit("Create user profile");
Console.WriteLine($"Committed: {hash}");

// View the result
var json = doc.HydrateJson();
Console.WriteLine(json);

// Check document state
Console.WriteLine($"Actor ID: {doc.ActorId}");
Console.WriteLine($"Pending operations: {doc.PendingOperations}");
```

# Understanding Actor IDs

Each document has an actor ID that uniquely identifies who made changes:

```csharp
var actorId = doc.ActorId;
Console.WriteLine($"This document's actor: {actorId}");
```

Automerge generates a random actor ID automatically. You can set a custom actor ID:

```csharp
doc.ActorId = "user-alice-device-1";
```

Actor IDs become important when syncing documents. They help Automerge track which peer made which changes.

# Working with Nested Structures

You can create nested maps and lists:

```csharp
using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
var root = doc.RootId;

// Create a list of tags
var tags = doc.PutObject(root, "tags", AutomergeContainerType.List);
doc.ListInsertString(tags, 0, "developer");
doc.ListInsertString(tags, 1, "golang");
doc.ListInsertString(tags, 2, "rust");

// Create a nested map for address
var address = doc.PutObject(root, "address", AutomergeContainerType.Map);
doc.PutString(address, "city", "San Francisco");
doc.PutString(address, "country", "USA");

doc.Commit("Add tags and address");

var json = doc.HydrateJson();
Console.WriteLine(json);
```

This creates a structure like:

```json
{
  "tags": ["developer", "golang", "rust"],
  "address": {
    "city": "San Francisco",
    "country": "USA"
  }
}
```

